%I A400939 #30 Oct 12 2026 15:42:50
%S A400939 1,3,6,10,5,11,18,9,18,28,14,7,20,34,17,33,50,25,44,22,43,65,88,112,
%T A400939 56,82,41,69,98,49,80,40,73,107,142,71,108,54,27,67,108,150,75,119,
%U A400939 164,210,105,153,202,101,152,76,38,19,74,37,94,47,106
%N A400939 The halving Recaman walk: a(1) = 1; for n > 1, a(n) = a(n-1)/2 if a(n-1) is even and a(n-1)/2 has not already appeared, otherwise a(n) = a(n-1) + n.
%C A400939 This is Recaman's sequence A005132 with "subtract n" replaced by "halve".
%C A400939 Conjecture: every positive integer appears. Note: 5091 is still missing after 4*10^10.
%C A400939 [Comment from _Benjamin Chaffin_, Oct 12 2026: a(111647551484) = 5091]
%C A400939 Unlike Recaman's sequence A005132, this sequence seems to admit no shortcut for computing distant terms. In A005132 the visited numbers form long runs of consecutive integers, which allows whole blocks of terms to be skipped. In this sequence visited numbers come in runs of only about 1.68 consecutive integers on average (measured up to 10^10 terms), and roughly 45% of the steps are halvings whose outcome depends on which numbers were visited earlier, so the only known way to obtain a(n) is to compute all earlier terms. Whether a(n) can be computed in space or time polynomial in log n is an open question.
%H A400939 Zlatko Damijanic, <a href="/A400939/b400939.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%t A400939 Module[{s, a = 1}, s[_] := False; s[a] = True; Join[{a}, Table[s[a = If[OddQ[a] || s[a/2], a + n, a/2]] = True; a, {n, 2, 100}]]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 11 2026 *)
%o A400939 (Python)
%o A400939 def aupto(n):
%o A400939     s, p, out = {1}, 1, [1]
%o A400939     for i in range(2, n+1):
%o A400939         p = p//2 if p%2 == 0 and p//2 not in s else p+i
%o A400939         s.add(p); out.append(p)
%o A400939     return out
%Y A400939 Cf. A005132, A400940.
%K A400939 nonn,look,new
%O A400939 1,2
%A A400939 _Zlatko Damijanic_, Oct 10 2026
